{\plain \fs36 \i\f3\fs36 Concerning the Service}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 In the Daily Office, the term "Officiant" is used to denote the person,\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 clerical or lay, who leads the Office. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 It is appropriate that other persons be assigned to read the Lessons, \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 and to lead other parts of the service not assigned to the officiant.  \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 The bishop, when present, appropriately concludes the Office with a \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 blessing. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 At celebrations of the Holy Eucharist, the Order for Morning or Evening\par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Prayer may be used in place of all that precedes the Offertory. \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 \f3\fs20 Additional Directions are on page 141. \par
}{\plain \fs24 \f3 \par
